  "body": "Owner directive (this task: `task-20260805-131351-merge-veridian-deterministic-ocid-master`).\n\n**Parent references:** `UMR-20260804-170055-a069` (canonical OCID-068 UMR, real status `completed`), `UMR-20260805-032731-b412` (OCID-068 permanent closure record, real status `completed`, PR #52 merge commit `c46da9b777e2a8a60e15230dacd72f2329e885af`).\n\nPhase 2 of the two phases `OCID_MASTER_STANDARD_V6_PHASE1_2026-08-05.md` (PR #54) proposed to Owner/PM: \"lifecycle state machine + registry integrity checks.\" Same narrow-PR discipline as Phase 1 and OCID-068's seven guardrail rules (each its own PR: #26, #29, #30, #32, #33, #34, #35) -- a real, reviewable, independently-tested increment, not one unreviewable mega-PR trying to build the full directive at once.\n\nFull writeup: `OCID_MASTER_STANDARD_V6_PHASE2_2026-08-05.md` (in this PR).\n\n## What this delivers\n\n1. **The real 11-state lifecycle machine** -- `OCID_LIFECYCLE_STATES` / `OCID_LIFECYCLE_TRANSITIONS` / `validate_lifecycle_transition()`: `created → registered → dispatched → running → testing → pull_request_created → merged → verified → closed`, `failed` reachable from any non-terminal state, `rolled_back` reachable only from `failed`, `closed`/`rolled_back` terminal. No illegal transitions.\n2. **Real durable transitions** -- new `ocid_lifecycle_state` table + `transition_ocid_lifecycle_state()`: refuses illegal transitions AND a second-UMR-for-the-same-OCID attempt (operationalizing \"never mint a second UMR for the same unit of work\"), both refusal paths durably audited via the existing `ocid_master_standard_audit_log`.\n3. **Real checkpoint resume** -- `resume_ocid_lifecycle()`: same OCID+UMR reused, never a restart from zero. Covered by a dedicated test that drives an OCID partway through the lifecycle, \"interrupts,\" then resumes and asserts the exact real checkpoint.\n4. **Real registry integrity checks** -- `check_registry_integrity()` + `establish_ocid_registry_schema_baseline()`: `schema_version_ok`, `checksum_ok` (sha256 over live DDL vs. an explicitly-established baseline -- never auto-established), `foreign_keys_ok` (`PRAGMA foreign_key_check`), `orphan_rows_ok`, `duplicate_index_ok`.\n5. **`build_step_result_contract()`** -- the specific, scoped primitive the Owner directive names for the output-contract test requirement: forces every step at/after a real failure point to `False`, never omitted.\n6. 3 new CLI subcommands: `transition-ocid-lifecycle`, `resume-ocid-lifecycle`, `check-registry-integrity`.\n\n## Deliberately still deferred\n\nOwnership-chain resolution, universal artifact graph, canonical-component discovery/locking as a generic mechanism, broader bootstrap sequencing, and the full strict-JSON-only automated output contract. See the doc's \"Deliberately still deferred\" section -- proposed as Phase 3, not decided unilaterally.\n\n## Tests\n\n`tests/test_ocid_master_standard_phase2.py` -- 20 new real tests (full legal sequential path, illegal-skip rejection, failure-from-any-state, rollback-only-from-failed, terminal-state rejection, real DB write + audit event on legal transitions, real refusal + audit event on illegal transitions and second-UMR attempts, a real resume test reusing the same OCID and UMR, all five integrity dimensions including real drift/orphan/duplicate-index detection cases, and the failure-forces-later-steps-false output contract requirement).\n\nFull repo suite: **122 passed, zero regressions**.\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)",
